{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1901 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ebd78aac4decbc58abc8f48b50d3a6806a03f9486bad0e1c006669bb6b6572d6" :edb/db-before-t 1900 :edb/db-after-t 1901 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535213 50 #inst "2026-10-13T04:13:32.070Z" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186052076 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052077 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186052076 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052078 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186052076 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052079 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186052076 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052080 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186052076 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052081 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186052076 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480114 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186052076 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480152 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186052076 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480326 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186052076 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480504 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186052076 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480507 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186052076 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480504 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186052851 1078 #edb/ref 17592186052852 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186053835 1078 #edb/ref 17592186053836 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054351 1071 "Simplify $a^{9}\\cdot a^{7}$." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186054351 1071 "Simplify $a^{9}\\cdot a^{7}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054351 1074 "We add the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{9} \\cdot a^{7} &=  \\\\\na^{9 + 7} &=  \\\\\n&= a^{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186054351 1074 "We add the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{9}\\cdot {a}^{7} & = \\\\ {a}^{9+7} & = \\\\ {a}^{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054352 1074 "We add the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} \\cdot x^{3} \\cdot x^{4} &=  \\\\\nx^{2 + 3 + 4} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $x^{n}=x^9$, so $n=9$." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186054352 1074 "We add the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{4} & = \\\\ {x}^{2+3+4} & = \\\\ {x}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $x^{n}=x^9$, so $n=9.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054353 1071 "Simplify $x\\left(x^3 y^5\\right) \\left(x^2 y^9\\right)$." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186054353 1071 "Simplify $x\\left(x^3 y^5\\right) \\left(x^2 y^9\\right).$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054353 1074 "Here we have two variables, $x$ and $y$. We will start by grouping the two variables separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x^{3}y^{5})(x^{2}y^{9}) &=  \\\\\nx^{1} \\cdot x^{3} \\cdot y^{5} \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y^{9} &=  \\\\\n&= (x^{1} \\cdot x^{3} \\cdot x^{2})(y^{5} \\cdot y^{9})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis way, we can apply the product rule to the two variables independently:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{1} \\cdot x^{3} \\cdot x^{2})(y^{5} \\cdot y^{9}) &=  \\\\\nx^{(1 + 3 + 2)} \\cdot y^{(5 + 9)} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{6}y^{14}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186054353 1074 "Here we have two variables, $x$ and $y.$ We will start by grouping the two variables separately: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{3}{y}^{5})({x}^{2}{y}^{9}) & = \\\\ {x}^{1}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\cdot {y}^{5}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{9} & = \\\\ ({x}^{1}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})({y}^{5}\\cdot {y}^{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis way, we can apply the product rule to the two variables independently:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{1}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})({y}^{5}\\cdot {y}^{9}) & = \\\\ {x}^{(1+3+2)}\\cdot {y}^{(5+9)} & = \\\\ {x}^{6}{y}^{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054396 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n(-3)^{3}_(⏟)_(-27) \\div 6 = (-27) \\div 6\n$$\n\nThen, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-27) \\div 6 &=  \\\\\n((-27))/(6) &=  \\\\\n- \\frac{27}{6} &=  \\\\\n- \\frac{27 \\div 3}{6 \\div 3} &=  \\\\\n&= - \\frac{9}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186054396 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(-3)^{3}}_{⏟}}_{-27}÷6=(-27)÷6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-27)÷6 & = \\\\ \\frac{(-27)}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{27}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{27÷3}{6÷3} & = \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054397 1071 "Evaluate $(1-4)^2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{12}$." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186054397 1071 "Evaluate $(1-4)^2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{12}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054397 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 - 4_(⏟)_(-3))^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{12} &= (-3)^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3)^{2}_(⏟)_(9) \\cdot \\frac{1}{12} &= 9 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{9}{1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{12} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{9 \\cdot 1}{1 \\cdot 12} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{9}{12} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{9 \\div 3}{12 \\div 3} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186054397 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({{1-4}_{⏟}}_{-3})^{2}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & =(-3)^{2}\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(-3)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{9}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & =9\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\times \\frac{1}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9\\times 1}{1\\times 12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9÷3}{12÷3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054398 1071 "What is the value of $\\left( 5 \\div (-2)^2 \\right) \\times \\left( -\\dfrac{4}{5} \\right).$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054398 1071 "What is the value of $\\left(5 \\div (-2)^2 \\right) \\times \\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5} \\right)$." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186054398 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, consider the expression inside the parenthesis:\n\n$$\n5 \\div (-2)^{2}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 \\div (-2)^{2} &=  \\\\\n5 \\div 4 &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5 \\div (-2)^{2}^(⏞)^(\\frac{5}{4})) \\cdot (-\\frac{4}{5}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{5}{4} \\cdot (-\\frac{4}{5}) &=  \\\\\n- \\frac{5}{4} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5} &=  \\\\\n- \\frac{5 \\cdot 4}{4 \\cdot 5} &=  \\\\\n- \\frac{5 \\cdot 4}{4 \\cdot 5} &=  \\\\\n&= - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186054398 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, consider the expression inside the parenthesis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2} & = \\\\ 5÷4 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{\\frac{5}{4}}{\\overset{⏞}{5÷(-2)^{2}}})\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) & = \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{4}{5} & = \\\\ -\\frac{5\\times 4}{4\\times 5} & = \\\\ -\\frac{\\cancel{5}\\times \\cancel{4}}{\\cancel{4}\\times \\cancel{5}} & = \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054606 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480662 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480652 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480695 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054608 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480665 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054608 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480674 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480700 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054704 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480642 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054704 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480646 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054705 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480672 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480640 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480638 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054876 1050 480.0 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186054921 1050 420.0 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186055118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186055118 1071 "Use exponents to rewrite the product $b \\cdot b^{2} \\cdot c^{3} \\cdot b^{4} \\cdot c^{5} \\cdot c^{6} \\cdot b^{7}$." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186055118 1071 "Use exponents to rewrite the product $b\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {c}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6}\\cdot {b}^{7}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186055118 1074 "Here we have two variables, $b$ and $c.$ We will start by grouping the two variables separately: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {c}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6}\\cdot {b}^{7} & = \\\\ {b}^{1}\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {b}^{7}\\cdot {c}^{3}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6} & = \\\\ ({b}^{1}\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {b}^{7})\\cdot ({c}^{3}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis way, we can apply the product rule to the two variables independently:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({b}^{1}\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {b}^{7})\\cdot ({c}^{3}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6}) & = \\\\ {b}^{(1+2+4+7)}\\cdot {c}^{(3+5+6)} & = \\\\ {b}^{14}{c}^{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186055269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186055269 1071 "${(-3)}^{3}+{4}^{3}=$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186055269 1071 "What is $(-3)^{3} + 4^{3}$?" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186055269 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. \n\nFirst, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-3)}^{3}+{4}^{3}=(-27)+64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-27)+64 & = \\\\ -27+64 & = \\\\ 37\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186055433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186055433 1071 "Find $n$ such that  ${z}^{5}\\cdot {z}^{7}\\cdot {z}^{2}={z}^{n}$  is correct." 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186055433 1071 "Find $n$ such that $z^{5} \\cdot z^{7} \\cdot z^{2} = z^{n}$ is correct." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186055433 1074 "We add the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{5}\\cdot {z}^{7}\\cdot {z}^{2} & = \\\\ {z}^{5+7+2} & = \\\\ {z}^{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore ${z}^{n}={z}^{14}$, so $n=14.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186055948 1064 "The **product rule for exponents** states that when we multiply two or more exponents that have the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\na^n\\cdot a^m=a^{n+m}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\na^2\\cdot a^3 = a^{2+3} = a^5\n$$\n\nTo see why the product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nFor the two factors $a^2$ and $a^3$ we have\n\n$$\na^2=a\\cdot a\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\na^3= a\\cdot a\\cdot a\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute these two expressions into $a^2\\cdot a^3$, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a^2\\cdot a^3 \\\\ &= (a\\cdot a) \\cdot (a\\cdot a\\cdot a) \\\\ &= a\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a \\\\ &= a^5 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product rule is just a shortcut for the process above." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186055948 1064 "The **product rule for exponents** states that when we multiply two or more exponents that have the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\na^n\\cdot a^m=a^{n+m}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\na^2\\cdot a^3 = a^{2+3} = a^5.\n$$\n\nTo see why the product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nFor the two factors $a^2$ and $a^3$ we have $a^2=a\\cdot a$ and $a^3= a\\cdot a\\cdot a.$ If we now substitute these two expressions into $a^2\\cdot a^3$, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na^2\\cdot a^3&=\\\n (a\\cdot a) \\cdot (a\\cdot a\\cdot a)&=\\\na\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a&=\\\na^5\n}\n$$\n\nThe product rule is just a shortcut for the process above." 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186056019 1064 "As we already know, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we must follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with negative numbers. For example, let's compute the value of\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times (-3)^2\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n(-2)\\,\\times\\overbrace{(-3)^2}^{\\large\\color{blue} 9} = (-2) \\times {\\color{blue} 9}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply:\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times 9 = -18\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times (-3)^2 = -18\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186056019 1064 "As we already know, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we must follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with negative numbers. For example, let's compute the value of\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times (-3)^2.\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.  First, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n(-2)\\,\\times\\overbrace{(-3)^2}^{\\large\\color{blue} 9}  = (-2) \\times {\\color{blue} 9}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we multiply:\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times 9 = -18\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times (-3)^2 = -18.\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186059422 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n5(5^2) = 5^1 \\times 5^2\n$$\n\nSo our expression can be written as\n$5^1 \\times 5^2 \\times 5^4$\n\nTo multiply powers of the same base, we simply add the exponents:\n\n$$\n5^1 \\times 5^2 \\times 5^4 = 5^{1+2+4} = 5^7\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186059422 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n5(5^2) = 5^1 \\times 5^2.\n$$\n\nSo our expression can be written as\n\n$$\n5^1 \\times 5^2 \\times 5^4\n$$\n\nTo multiply powers of the same base, we simply add the exponents:\n\n$$\n5^1 \\times 5^2 \\times 5^4 = 5^{1+2+4} = 5^7\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186059423 1074 "To multiply powers of the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2\\times \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 =  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{2+2} =  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^4\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186059423 1074 "To multiply powers of the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2\\times \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{2+2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^4\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186059424 1071 "Evaluate $2^2\\times 2^{-5}$." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186059424 1071 "Evaluate $2^2\\times 2^{-5}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186059424 1074 "To multiply powers of the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\n2^2\\times 2^{-5} =2^{2+(-5)} = 2^{-3}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $2^{-3}$, we find the reciprocal of the base $(2)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n2^{-3} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^3 =\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186059424 1074 "To multiply powers of the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\n2^2\\times 2^{-5} =2^{2+(-5)} = 2^{-3}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $2^{-3},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(2)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n2^{-3}  = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^3 =\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186059871 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480648 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186059871 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480650 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186059872 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480679 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186059872 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480710 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186059873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480635 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186059873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480678 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186059942 1050 420.0 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186060989 1064 "To multiply powers of the same base, we simply *add* the exponents. This is called the **product rule for exponents**.\nFor example, in the expression\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3})$. Therefore, we can simplify this expression by adding the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{4+2} = {\\color{blue}3}^6\n$$\n\n**Note:** The product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{4} \\cdot 3^{2} &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3_(⏟)_(4copies) \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3_(⏟)_(2copies) &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3_(⏟)_(6copies) &=  \\\\\n&= 3^{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186060989 1064 "To multiply powers of the same base, we simply *add* the exponents. This is called the **product rule for exponents**.\nFor example, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3}).$ Therefore, we can simplify this expression by adding the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{4+2} = {\\color{blue}3}^6\n$$\n\n**Note:** The product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4}\\times {3}^{2} & = \\\\ {{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}}\\times {{3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{6\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186060990 1064 "We can use the product rule for exponents with negative exponents too. For instance, to simplify $3^3\\times 3^{-5}$, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\n3^{3} \\times 3^{-5} = 3^{3-5} = 3^{-2}\n$$\n\nNow, to evaluate $3^{-2}$, we find the reciprocal of the base $(3)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n3^{-2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186060990 1064 "We can use the product rule for exponents with negative exponents too. For instance, to simplify $3^3\\times 3^{-5},$ we add the exponents:\n\n$$\n3^{3} \\times 3^{-5} = 3^{3-5} = 3^{-2}\n$$\n\nNow, to evaluate $3^{-2},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(3)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n3^{-2}  = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186064328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480664 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186064341 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480655 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186067126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067128 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186067126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067129 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186067126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067130 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186067126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480194 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186067126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480320 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186067126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480453 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186067126 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480194 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186067858 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067859 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186067858 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067860 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186067858 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067861 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186067858 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067862 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186067858 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067863 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186067858 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480127 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186067858 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480232 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186067858 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480234 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186067858 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480361 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186067858 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480459 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186067858 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480459 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068517 1074 "The numerator and denominator have no common factors, so the expression can not be reduced. The expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+2}{2x}\n$$\n\nis already in lowest terms." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186068517 1074 "The numerator and denominator have no common factors, so the expression can not be reduced. The expression \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+2}{2x}\n$$\n\n is already in lowest terms." 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068518 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2x + 4}{2} &= (2(x + 2))/(2) \\\\\n&= (2(x + 2))/(2 \\cdot 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x + 2}{1} \\\\\n&= x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186068518 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+4}{2} & =\\frac{2(x+2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}(x+2)}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{1} \\\\  & =x+2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068653 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac {6y + 2} {3xy + x}$." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186068653 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac {6y + 2} {3xy + x}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068653 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n    \\eqalign{\n        \\dfrac {6y + 2} {3xy + x} &= \\dfrac {6y + 2} {x(3y + 1)} \\[5pt] \n        &=\\dfrac {2(3y + 1)} {x(3y + 1)} \\[5pt] \n        &=\\dfrac {2\\cancel{(3y + 1)} } {x \\cancel{(3y + 1)} } \\[5pt] \n        &=\\dfrac {2 } {x}\n    }\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068653 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {6y + 2} {3xy + x} &= \\dfrac {6y + 2} {x(3y + 1)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {2(3y + 1)} {x(3y + 1)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {2\\cancel{(3y + 1)}} {x \\cancel{(3y + 1)}} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {2} {x} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186068654 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{5c + 10p - 20}{5p+15}$." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186068654 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{5c + 10p - 20}{5p+15}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068654 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5c + 10p - 20}{5p + 15} &= (5(c + 2p - 4))/(5(p + 3)) \\\\\n&= (5(c + 2p - 4))/(5(p + 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{c + 2p - 4}{p + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186068654 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n![](images/82/82b6ffda3164ec24147f28968a08f6d96bf64a2ec9aaf261f673c171dd1b1f40.svg)" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480701 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480645 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480703 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480705 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480685 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480699 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480681 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480706 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186068956 1050 540.0 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186069326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186069326 1071 "What is $\\frac{4ab - 8 + 10x}{10x + 8 - 2ab}$ reduced to its lowest terms?" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186069326 1071 "What is $\\frac{4ab-8+10x}{10x+8-2ab}$ reduced to its lowest terms?" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186069326 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4ab-8+10x}{10x+8-2ab} & =\\frac{2(2ab-4+5x)}{2(5x+4-ab)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}(2ab-4+5x)}{\\cancel{2}(5x+4-ab)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2ab-4+5x}{5x+4-ab}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186069448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186069448 1071 "What is $\\frac{8a - 4b + 12}{20b + 8a}$ reduced to its lowest terms?" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186069448 1071 "What is $\\frac{8a-4b+12}{20b+8a}$ reduced to its lowest terms?" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186069448 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8a-4b+12}{20b+8a} & =\\frac{4(2a-b+3)}{4(5b+2a)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{4}(2a-b+3)}{\\cancel{4}(5b+2a)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2a-b+3}{5b+2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186069927 1064 "To simplify a rational expression, we cancel out the largest common factor from the numerator and denominator. To do this, we sometimes need to factor the numerator and denominator before canceling.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to simplify\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4} &=\\dfrac {4(x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {\\cancel{2} \\cdot 2(x - 3)} {\\cancel{2}(5x + 2)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {2(x - 3)} {5x + 2} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186069927 1064 "To simplify a rational expression, we cancel out the largest common factor from the numerator and denominator. To do this, we sometimes need to factor the numerator and denominator before canceling.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to simplify $\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}.$ To do this, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {4(x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\t\t\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {\\cancel{2} \\cdot 2(x - 3)} {\\cancel{2}(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\t\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {2(x - 3)} {5x + 2}\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186078472 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480636 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186078531 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480647 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186102542 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102547 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186105490 1071 "Let ![](images/bf/bf51b89da6e28b6f6cf620b709298b81b8c202b54a601741b0bed05cd265b3e9.svg) and ![](images/39/39895d1db9365c174ca95dcbd4ba9d374a1c14929859f652e3cfdd07993569ac.svg) Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186105490 1071 "Let ![](images/e4/e477b14c9148f4132c747a358e1e99f76d40df85b82c66d6e7dd38581b33913c.png) and ![](images/b1/b17d86e3e395b2fd752f5529090c0a5e4e2fdce17642be5594bee98e8c47fed2.png) Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186105701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480688 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186105702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480692 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186105703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480639 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186105916 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480656 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186105916 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480689 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186105962 1050 720.0 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186106111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186106111 1071 "Suppose that $\\mid a|= 8$, $\\mid b|= 2$ and that the angle between the vectors is $θ = 120^{∘}$. Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b$ in terms of the vector $b$." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186106111 1071 "Suppose that $|\\,a\\,|=8$, $|\\,b\\,|=2$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta ={120}^{∘}.$ Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b$ in terms of the vector $b.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186106111 1074 "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $b:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{b}{|\\,b\\,|}=\\frac{b}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}b\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}b \\\\  & =-2b\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186107174 1064 "The **scalar projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the distance that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nTo illustrate the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b},$ consider the point $P$ on the line that contains $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd37bdb2a97b4125fb360990d3c4a36b48bcb50433de033a154799db00e474dd.png)\n\nThe length $OP$ is called the **scalar projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or the **component** of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b}$). It is denoted as $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and we can compute it using cosine in $\\triangle OPA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =OP \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula is the general definition of the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186107174 1064 "The **scalar projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the distance that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nTo illustrate the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b},$ consider the point $P$ on the line that contains $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd37bdb2a97b4125fb360990d3c4a36b48bcb50433de033a154799db00e474dd.png)\n\nThe length $OP$ is called the **scalar projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or the **component** of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b}$). It is denoted as $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and we can compute it using cosine in $\\triangle OPA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =OP \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula is the general definition of the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186107175 1064 "We have been using the following formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nHowever, sometimes we are not given the angle between two vectors. In such cases, we can solve for $\\cos \\theta$ in terms of the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our original formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\cdot \\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186107175 1064 "We have been using the following formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nHowever, sometimes we are not given the angle between two vectors. In such cases, we can solve for $\\cos \\theta$ in terms of the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our original formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\cdot \\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186107177 1064 "The scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ can be negative. This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c19648ea2df1935e89e10df2f7f5ab33ad537a5fdb52bb97c3555c9a0365701e.png)\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ where it is known that \n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2, \\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ.\n$$\n\nNotice that the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors is obtuse. \n\nComputing the scalar projection of $\\mathbf a$ onto $\\mathbf b$ using the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}