ied Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators." 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186166561 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=6x(1-0.1x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-7y+5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186166561 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\nx' = 6x(1-0.1x) - 3xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $6x(1-0.1x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-3xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y = 0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\nx' = 6x(1-0.1x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x(1-0.1x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=10.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $10.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186166561 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\nx' = 6x(1-0.1x) - 3xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $6x(1-0.1x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-3xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y = 0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\nx' = 6x(1-0.1x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x(1-0.1x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=10.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $10.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186166926 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480663 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186166926 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480668 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186166926 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480698 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186166927 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480667 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186166927 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480707 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186166928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480691 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186166929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480649 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186166929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480690 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186166960 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480675 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186166960 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480684 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186167005 1050 1380.0 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186167260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186167260 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186167260 1071 "${x^{′} = 2x(1 - 0.2x) - xy; y^{′} =-4y + 2xy$\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x = x(t)$ represents the prey and $y = y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:$(0, 0), ($ {{field-1}} $, 0), ($ {{field-2}} $$, {{field-3}} $)$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186167260 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.2x)-xy=0 \\\\ -4y+2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+2x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.2x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(5,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)(1-0.2(2))-(2)y & =0 \\\\ 2.4-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1.2).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(5,0),\\,(2,1.2).\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186167261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480564 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186167261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480582 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186167261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480592 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186167261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186167261 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=8x(1-0.75x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\,2]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186167261 1071 "${x^{′} = 8x(1 - 0.75x) - xy; y^{′} =-4y + 4xy$\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x = x(t)$ represents the prey and $y = y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium $x^{∗} = [1, 2]^{T}$, we get\n\n| $J(x^{∗}) =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $$. |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186167261 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=8x(1-0.75x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-4y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8x(1-0.75x)-xy)=8-12x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8x(1-0.75x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4y+4xy)=-4+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}8-12x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -4+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}8-12(1)-(2) & -(1) \\\\ 4(2) & -4+4(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 8 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-8(-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Since both eigenvalues are real and negative, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186167292 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ -{x}^{2}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=2.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=2.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186167292 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ -{x}^{2}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=2.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=2.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186168015 1064 "The Lotka–Volterra predator-prey model with **carrying capacity** is given by \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, \n\n- $x=x(t)$ is the *prey population*, and\n\n- $y=y(t)$ is the *predator population*.\n\nThe only difference from the Lotka–Volterra model is the factor $(1-hx)$ in the prey equation. Notice that if $h=0,$ we get the standard model:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our modified version of the model, the *intrinsic growth* of prey is given by the $ax(1-hx)$ term. Now, if there are no predators ($y=0$), then \n\n$$\nx' = ax(1-hx).\n$$\n\nRecall that general solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax(1-hx)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,\\frac{1}{h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{1}{h}.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the *carrying capacity* (the limit the number of prey is approaching as $t$ goes to infinity).\n\n![](images/8b/8b7a6d12af77f6c2d2e6d3596e71a51a3d697a5c28e9f9c64c5675a01df812d5.png)" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186168015 1064 "The Lotka–Volterra predator-prey model with **carrying capacity** is given by $${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{'}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}$$ where $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here,\n\n- $x=x(t)$ is the *prey population*, and\n- $y=y(t)$ is the *predator population*.\n\nThe only difference from the Lotka–Volterra model is the factor $(1-hx)$ in the prey equation. Notice that if $h=0,$ we get the standard model: $${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{'}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIn our modified version of the model, the *intrinsic growth* of prey is given by the $ax(1-hx)$ term. Now, if there are no predators ($y=0$), then $$x' = ax(1-hx).$$\n\nRecall that general solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation: $$\\begin{aligned}ax(1-hx)=0\\,\\Rightarrow \\,x=0,\\,\\frac{1}{h}\\end{aligned}$$ So, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{1}{h}.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the *carrying capacity* (the limit the number of prey is approaching as $t$ goes to infinity).\n\n![](images/8b/8b7a6d12af77f6c2d2e6d3596e71a51a3d697a5c28e9f9c64c5675a01df812d5.png)" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186168016 1064 "Recall that the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas equilibria at\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nNotice that the second component of the third equilibrium point is positive only when \n\n$$\nh < \\dfrac{d}{c},\n$$\n\nwhich guarantees that the third fixed point will lie in the first quadrant. For now, let's consider only this case.\n\nWe now evaluate the Jacobian matrix\n![](images/89/89ca57d2d308201a46a31695c27ad866604ef4f230609ad3047e186824e5dc28.svg)\nat the nonzero equilibrium ![](images/f2/f2965d33d48e48fa977e876803aa3da55c1c47c3569a24cb8341b97ba43420c2.svg) to get\n\n![](images/b2/b239f1170a7bf5a5f6ad6043492aa0772adcc2a53e7f3ebdad15ad195b1520d9.svg)\n\nBecause we are considering the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = -a < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 = \\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an *unstable saddle*.\n\nAnalyzing the type of the third equilibrium in full generality becomes considerably more cumbersome. Therefore, we'll examine a concrete example later in the lesson. For now, we simply note that the third equilibrium is an *asymptotically stable (spiral) sink*.\n\nA typical phase portrait of the system, in the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c82332ed5277586a2b97e900383619a5d61cdb73ff4b60a92de3d5460165165.png)\n\nThe global picture is the following:\n\n- There are two straight-line types of solutions:\n\n- Along the $x$-axis, trajectories approach the equilibrium at $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$ Indeed, in the absence of the predators, the prey approaches the carrying capacity.\n\n- Along the $y$-axis, trajectories approach the origin. Indeed, in the absence of the prey, the predators decrease exponentially.\n\n- The curved trajectories converge to the equilibrium point $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).$\n\nThe corresponding population dynamics graphs look as depicted below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7673170825344e1831f2ba01ce4c20f10091380ff4e116f3b6bd9e7666e51aa.png)" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186168016 1064 "Recall that the system $${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{'}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}$$ has equilibria at $$(0,0), \\qquad \\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right), \\qquad \\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).$$\n\nNotice that the second component of the third equilibrium point is positive only when $$h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$$ which guarantees that the third fixed point will lie in the first quadrant. For now, let's consider only this case.\n\nWe now evaluate the Jacobian matrix $$J = [\\begin{aligned}a(1-2hx)-by & -bx \\\\ dy & -c+dx\\end{aligned}]$$ at the nonzero equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast = [\\begin{aligned}1/h \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$J(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast) = [\\begin{aligned}a(1-2h(\\frac{1}{h}))-b(0) & -b(\\frac{1}{h}) \\\\ d(0) & -c+d(\\frac{1}{h})\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}-a & -\\frac{b}{h} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{c}{h}(\\frac{d}{c}-h)\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nBecause we are considering the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ we have that $$\\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = -a < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 = \\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an *unstable saddle*.\n\nAnalyzing the type of the third equilibrium in full generality becomes considerably more cumbersome. Therefore, we'll examine a concrete example later in the lesson. For now, we simply note that the third equilibrium is an *asymptotically stable (spiral) sink*.\n\nA typical phase portrait of the system, in the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c82332ed5277586a2b97e900383619a5d61cdb73ff4b60a92de3d5460165165.png)\n\nThe global picture is the following:\n\n- There are two straight-line types of solutions:\n\n- Along the $x$-axis, trajectories approach the equilibrium at $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$ Indeed, in the absence of the predators, the prey approaches the carrying capacity.\n- Along the $y$-axis, trajectories approach the origin. Indeed, in the absence of the prey, the predators decrease exponentially.\n- The curved trajectories converge to the equilibrium point $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).$\n\nThe corresponding population dynamics graphs look as depicted below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7673170825344e1831f2ba01ce4c20f10091380ff4e116f3b6bd9e7666e51aa.png)" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186168017 1064 "Again, recall that the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas equilibria at\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nWe have already considered the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ which guarantees that the third equilibrium will lie in the first quadrant. Let's now consider the remaining cases.\n\n- When $h = \\dfrac{d}{c},$ the third equilibrium coincides with the second one. So, we have only two equilibria:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right) = \\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, 0\\right)\n$$\n\n- When $h > \\dfrac{d}{c},$ the third equilibrium is in the fourth quadrant (its $y$-coordinate is negative). So, we have only two equilibria that make sense in the context of the model:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nHence, we get only two equilibria. \n\nThe trajectories near the origin remain similar. However, the equilibrium at\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nwill be an *asymptotically stable sink*.\n\nA typical phase portrait of the system, in the case when $h \\geq \\dfrac{d}{c},$ looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b94beebb63bc1312d56f655e48b3bc7cdb1b9badc5cdacd6b88b073c5055adc9.png)\n\nThe global picture is the following:\n\n- There are two straight-line types of solutions:\n\n- Along the $x$-axis, trajectories approach the equilibrium at $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$ Indeed, in the absence of the predators, the prey approaches the carrying capacity.\n\n- Along the $y$-axis, trajectories approach the origin. Indeed, in the absence of the prey, the predators decrease exponentially.\n\n- The curved trajectories converge to the equilibrium point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right).$\n\nThe corresponding population dynamics graphs look as depicted below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd60f32a5daff3c805b097d121814d19e04781ed1bcfead989cde82db674e5d9.png)\n\nEventually, the *predators go extinct* while the prey approaches the carrying capacity of the system.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186168017 1064 "Again, recall that the system $${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{'}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}$$ has equilibria at $$(0,0), \\qquad \\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right), \\qquad \\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).$$\n\nWe have already considered the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ which guarantees that the third equilibrium will lie in the first quadrant. Let's now consider the remaining cases.\n\n- When $h = \\dfrac{d}{c},$ the third equilibrium coincides with the second one. So, we have only two equilibria: $$(0,0), \\qquad \\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right) = \\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, 0\\right)$$\n- When $h > \\dfrac{d}{c},$ the third equilibrium is in the fourth quadrant (its $y$-coordinate is negative). So, we have only two equilibria that make sense in the context of the model: $$(0,0), \\qquad \\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right)$$\n\nHence, we get only two equilibria.\n\nThe trajectories near the origin remain similar. However, the equilibrium at $$\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right)$$ will be an *asymptotically stable sink*.\n\nA typical phase portrait of the system, in the case when $h \\geq \\dfrac{d}{c},$ looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b94beebb63bc1312d56f655e48b3bc7cdb1b9badc5cdacd6b88b073c5055adc9.png)\n\nThe global picture is the following:\n\n- There are two straight-line types of solutions:\n\n- Along the $x$-axis, trajectories approach the equilibrium at $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$ Indeed, in the absence of the predators, the prey approaches the carrying capacity.\n- Along the $y$-axis, trajectories approach the origin. Indeed, in the absence of the prey, the predators decrease exponentially.\n- The curved trajectories converge to the equilibrium point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right).$\n\nThe corresponding population dynamics graphs look as depicted below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd60f32a5daff3c805b097d121814d19e04781ed1bcfead989cde82db674e5d9.png)\n\nEventually, the *predators go extinct* while the prey approaches the carrying capacity of the system.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186168028 1064 "Recall that the equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, for the Lotka–Volterra model \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe equilibria are the solutions of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}ax(1-hx)-bxy=0 \\\\ -cy+dxy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-cy+dxy & =0 \\\\ y(-c+dx) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=\\dfrac{c}{d}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax(1-hx)-bx(0) & =0 \\\\ ax(1-hx) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{1}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$\n\n- If $x=\\dfrac{c}{d},$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{c}{d})(1-h(\\frac{c}{d}))-b(\\frac{c}{d})y & =0 \\\\ a(1-\\frac{c}{d}h)-by & =0 \\\\ a(1-\\frac{c}{d}h) & =by \\\\ y & =\\frac{a}{b}(1-\\frac{c}{d}h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1-\\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1-\\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186168028 1064 "Recall that the equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, for the Lotka–Volterra model $${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{'}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}$$ the equilibria are the solutions of the following system of equations: $${\\begin{aligned}ax(1-hx)-bxy=0 \\\\ -cy+dxy=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-cy+dxy & =0 \\\\ y(-c+dx) & =0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=\\dfrac{c}{d}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have $$\\begin{aligned}ax(1-hx)-bx(0) & =0 \\\\ ax(1-hx) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{1}{h}.\\end{aligned}$$ Thus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$\n- If $x=\\dfrac{c}{d},$ substituting it into the first equation, we have $$\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{c}{d})(1-h(\\frac{c}{d}))-b(\\frac{c}{d})y & =0 \\\\ a(1-\\frac{c}{d}h)-by & =0 \\\\ a(1-\\frac{c}{d}h) & =by \\\\ y & =\\frac{a}{b}(1-\\frac{c}{d}h).\\end{aligned}$$ Thus, another equilibrium is $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1-\\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are $$(0,0), \\qquad \\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right), \\qquad \\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1-\\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186168029 1064 "Consider the modified Lotka–Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants, $x=x(t)$ is the prey population, and $y=y(t)$ is the predator population.\n\nTo classify the equilibrium at the origin, we need to find the corresponding Jacobian matrix.\n\nLet's denote $f_1(x,y)=ax(1-hx)-bxy$ and $f_2(x,y)=-cy+dxy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(ax(1\\,-\\,hx)-bxy)=a(1\\,-\\,2hx)-by, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(ax(1\\,-\\,hx)-bxy)=-bx, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-cy+dxy)=dy, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-cy+dxy)=-c+dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/1d/1db459c6fd9f3bad4e5f044d8de8a4e185aeecadddcd73fac75dff09e6fa607d.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/03/035a074e405004b43a3f5e509b46affa6e0e53dbf53e26e01149b7ae545c6f64.svg) to get\n\n![](images/cf/cf1eb1b02f6d0dd1b690ac3a69abd1ac6777f592c713060920ef1dcee3fb5600.svg)\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = a > 0$ and $\\lambda_2 = -c < 0.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an *unstable saddle*.\n\nThus, the equilibrium at the origin for this model behaves identically to that of the standard Lotka–Volterra model." 131