imes 2\\times 2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186175717 1071 "Calculate the value of $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{-2}$." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186175717 1071 "Calculate the value of $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{-2}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186175717 1074 "We find the reciprocal of the base\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 1 4\\right)\n$$\n\nand then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+{\\color{blue}2})$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1}{4})^{-2} &= (4)^{2} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186175717 1074 "We find the reciprocal of the base $\\left(\\dfrac 1 4\\right),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+{\\color{blue}2}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{-2} & =(4)^{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186175718 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{49}\n$$\n\nas a base raised to a power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{49} &= \\frac{1 \\cdot 1}{7 \\cdot 7} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7} \\cdot \\frac{1}{7} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{7})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)\n$$\n\nand then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{7} \\right)^2 = 7^{-2}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186175718 1074 "First, we write $\\dfrac{1}{49}$ as a base raised to a power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{49} & =\\frac{1\\times 1}{7\\times 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{7})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{1}{7} \\right)^2 = 7^{-2}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186175957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480671 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186175957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480683 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176020 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480643 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176020 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480661 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176020 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480676 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176021 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480644 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176021 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480660 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176021 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480677 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176021 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480697 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480693 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480708 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480657 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480654 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176102 1050 420.0 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176112 1050 420.0 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176306 1071 "The population $y(t)$ of turtles on a certain island follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1.5y-0.001{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=500,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of turtles?" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176306 1071 "The population $y(t)$ of turtles on a certain island follows the logistic growth model\n$y^{′} = 1.5y - 0.001y^{2}, y(0) = 500$,\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of turtles?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186176306 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1.5y-0.001{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.001y(1\\,500-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,500.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,500,$ and we conclude that the number of turtles cannot exceed $1\\,500.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176317 1074 "First, we write $\\frac{9}{4}$ as a base raised to a power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{3\\times 3}{2\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{3}{2}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{-2}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176389 1071 "The population $P(t)$ of squirrels in a certain forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,5P(6\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=1\\,000\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the squirrel population growing at the fastest possible rate?" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176389 1071 "The population $P(t)$ of squirrels in a certain forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$\\frac{dP}{dt} = 0.0005P(6000 - P), P(0) = 1000$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the squirrel population growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186176389 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFrom our equation, we see that $a=6\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $6\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\,000=3\\,000.\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176488 1071 "${(\\frac{1}{9})}^{-2}=$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186176488 1071 "What is $(\\frac{1}{9})^{-2}$?" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186176488 1074 "We find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{1}{9}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{-2} & =(9)^{2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186177034 1064 "Let's go back to the following logistic growth equation:\n\n$$\ny' = 5y(10-y), \\qquad y(0) = y_0.\n$$\n\nThe logistic growth curve for this problem is sketched below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a225fd04f81bd65e1bad3ff14097563d112a16e3847aac26cc86591533ea5d0.png)\n\nThe curve contains an inflection point, that is, a point where $y(t)$ is growing at its fastest possible rate. What information can we deduce about this point? \n\nTo find the inflection point, we solve $y''(t) = 0.$ First, we differentiate the differential equation using implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{''} & =(5y)^{'}\\cdot (10-y)+(5y)\\cdot (10-y)^{'} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (10-y)\\cdot {y}^{'}+(5y)\\cdot (-1)\\cdot {y}^{'} \\\\  & ={y}^{'}\\cdot (5\\cdot (10-y)+(5y)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}\\cdot (50-5y-5y) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}\\cdot (50-10y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to solve $y''(t) = 0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny'\\cdot\\left(50-10y\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe know that $y'$ is increasing for all $t,$ so $y'\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n50-10y = 0\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad y = 5.\n$$\n\nLet's mark this on our diagram.\n\n![](images/75/75d0363483e1bda70decb171466fe9e506eb0da80d0e78ed893bb61551ae9d44.png)\n\nThe fact that the inflection point occurs when $y$ is precisely half the carrying capacity is *not* a coincidence. In fact, this is always the case. The general situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7754f42adbb938c1d07ab2636a4c24f1cd0bc8536c974af2497e997589efa115.png)" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186177034 1064 "Let's go back to the following logistic growth equation:\n\n$$\ny' = 5y(10-y), \\qquad y(0) = y_0.\n$$\n\nThe logistic growth curve for this problem is sketched below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a225fd04f81bd65e1bad3ff14097563d112a16e3847aac26cc86591533ea5d0.png)\n\nThe curve contains an inflection point, that is, a point where $y(t)$ is growing at its fastest possible rate. What information can we deduce about this point? \n\nTo find the inflection point, we solve $y''(t) = 0.$ First, we differentiate the differential equation using implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =(5y)^{′}\\cdot (10-y)+(5y)\\cdot (10-y)^{′} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (10-y)\\cdot {y}^{′}+(5y)\\cdot (-1)\\cdot {y}^{′} \\\\  & ={y}^{′}\\cdot (5\\cdot (10-y)+(5y)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & ={y}^{′}\\cdot (50-5y-5y) \\\\  & ={y}^{′}\\cdot (50-10y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to solve $y''(t) = 0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny'\\cdot\\left(50-10y\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe know that $y'$ is increasing for all $t,$ so $y'\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n50-10y = 0\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad y = 5.\n$$\n\nLet's mark this on our diagram.\n\n![](images/75/75d0363483e1bda70decb171466fe9e506eb0da80d0e78ed893bb61551ae9d44.png)\n\nThe fact that the inflection point occurs when $y$ is precisely half the carrying capacity is *not* a coincidence. In fact, this is always the case. The general situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7754f42adbb938c1d07ab2636a4c24f1cd0bc8536c974af2497e997589efa115.png)" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186177087 1064 "We know how to find $2^{-1}$. But what about $2^{-2}, 2^{-3},2^{-4},\\ldots$?\n\nTo work out ${\\color{blue}{2}}^{{\\color{red}{-2}}}$, all we need to do is find the reciprocal of the base $({\\color{blue}{2}})$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $({{\\color{red}{+2}}})$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{-2} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2}) \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1 \\cdot 1}{2 \\cdot 2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA similar approach can be used for other powers. Let's see another example." 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186177087 1064 "We know how to find $2^{-1}.$ But what about $2^{-2}, 2^{-3},2^{-4},\\ldots$?\n\nTo work out ${\\color{blue}{2}}^{{\\color{red}{-2}}},$ all we need to do is find the reciprocal of the base $({\\color{blue}{2}})$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $({{\\color{red}{+2}}})$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2})\\times (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1\\times 1}{2\\times 2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA similar approach can be used for other powers. Let's see another example." 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186272022 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find the orthogonal projection of $a$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{b\\}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186272022 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find the orthogonal projection of $a$ onto $\\text{Span}{b}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186272022 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $a$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{b\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 8+1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 10+2\\cdot 6}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{8+4-10+12}{1+1+1+4}b \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{7}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186272022 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $a$ onto $\\text{Span}{b}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 8+1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 10+2\\cdot 6}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{8+4-10+12}{1+1+1+4}b \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{7}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186272023 1071 "Let $b=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]$ and $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{a\\}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 true] [17592186272023 1071 "Let $b=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]$ and $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto $\\text{Span}{a}.$" 13194139535213 17592186045417 false] [17592186272023 1074 "The vector projection of $b$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1\